/ref 1083 #uuid "fe3ce6a6-c3bd-565b-9cc1-a4620449adec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323165 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323165 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323165 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323165 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323165 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323165 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323165 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323165 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186321448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323165 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "001c22af-200a-5e74-a872-4fc6a4c58849" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323242 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323242 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323242 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323242 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323242 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323242 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323242 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323242 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186321596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323242 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "25ac795a-9483-57f9-a1e0-931dea19d10f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323646 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323646 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323646 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323646 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186323003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323646 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186323152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323646 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323646 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323646 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186322291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323646 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f1745976-de79-5730-b907-42992b1e0aa8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323912 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323912 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37399" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323912 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323912 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}21 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -12 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323912 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f559f7f4-e02a-5090-9ef8-87c06ed535b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323912 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -12 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -7\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 18{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{9}{10}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{20}(13{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(13(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323913 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323913 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323913 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323913 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323913 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b75fe299-3a00-56ce-ac2b-3a6b8472e075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323913 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(3)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{12}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323915 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323915 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323915 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323915 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323915 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "774c9d47-2d15-5219-81a5-dce5b7c23a5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323915 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that its augmented matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =6{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354852 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354852 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354852 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15027588-af4d-5a5b-882c-42ce50e05514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355007 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355007 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355007 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "290f4389-144e-58ae-8aa0-f8779961574c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355251 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355251 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355251 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "426fd857-13b1-5ab3-9758-9722e35fc330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355261 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355261 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #