d388c72-903e-5ac2-9ee0-117676cd3717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399194 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399194 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83481" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399194 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399194 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399194 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a11ee4d1-3e88-5212-80d0-293758418f1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399194 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-11{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -14{x}_{1}+11{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 9 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -11 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{11}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{2} & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{7}{8}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399195 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399195 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399195 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399195 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399195 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399195 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399195 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399195 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186399199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399195 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f7423f8b-a007-5757-8b22-bf2bd8ad4565" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399196 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399196 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399196 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5cf764b3-b5dc-5b5a-abc6-619ed240e8d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
